      "caveat": "Therapies are grouped into LINES before being sequenced, by the definition every line count here shares: systemic-therapy treatment records (class, from SUBTYPE or else TREATMENT_TYPE, on the systemic list: chemotherapy, immunotherapy, targeted, hormone, biologic, investigational and their spellings; radiation, surgery, bone and ancillary treatment are not lines). A record starting more than 90 days after every earlier one has stopped begins a new line; anything closer is the same line, and a line is labelled by its whole combination (sorted, so 'A + B' and 'B + A' are one regimen). That grouping is the point — a patient here has a median of 6 treatment records and 4 distinct agents, and combination therapy is several records describing ONE decision, not several lines. Lines are labelled by SUBTYPE. ENDING A PATH IS SPLIT IN TWO, because the difference matters more than anything else here: 'no further line' means the study kept watching for at least 90 days (one line gap, the same rule that defines a line) and saw no further therapy; 'record ends here' means it did not, so nothing can be concluded about that patient. 55.5% of paths end censored — reading those as patients who finished treatment would be a claim about the record-keeping dressed up as one about the patient. Neither terminal is an outcome measure. 51 less common regimens folded into 'other', which is 23.3% of first-line patients in the first panel — combination regimens have a genuinely long tail, so 'other' is real diversity rather than a rounding bucket. Raise topN to split it further. Both panels share one scale and one colour per regimen, so their heights are directly comparable. TP53 mut: 11,564 with a first line; TP53 wt: 9,645 with a first line.",
      "method": {
        "steps": [
          {
            "kind": "fetch",
